<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qiyan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as qayna, singing girls, singing slave girls</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The qiyān (singular qayna) were a social class of women trained as entertainers in the pre-modern Islamic world, who flourished under the Umayyad and Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Often called singing slave girls, they were trained in singing, dancing, composing music and verse, reciting anecdotes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, and included free women, among them the Abbasid princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulayya-bint-al-mahdi">Ulayya bint al-Mahdi</a>, as well as enslaved women, who were legally sexually available to their owners. A significant share of the medieval Arabic women poets whose work survives were qiyān. The institution declined with the waning fortunes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, as austerity left the arts unfunded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qiyan.jpg" alt="Qiyan" width="330" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, attribution Uploader Islami · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadîth_Bayâd_wa_Riyâd_-_BAV_Ar368_f10r_-_Garden_scene.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word is the feminine form of the pre-Islamic qayn, a blacksmith or craftsman, whose sense widened to manual labourers, then to people paid for their work, and finally to anyone performing an art for reward. Common English renderings miss that qiyān might be of any age; other translations include courtesans, musical concubines or women musicians. In some sources they form a subset of jawāri, female slaves, and are called slave-girl poets or songstresses. It is not clear how early the institution emerged, but it certainly flourished in the Abbasid period, when qiyān were often educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Literature often linked them with licentiousness, and sexuality was part of their appeal, yet they do not seem to have been sex workers, although common qiyān who performed for the public in qiyān houses sometimes worked in brothels. Ibrahim al-Mawsili is reported to have claimed that his father first trained light-skinned girls as qiyān and so raised their price, a claim of uncertain truth. Their prominence makes them one of the most richly recorded groups of women in the pre-modern Islamicate world, through a treatise by al-Jahiz, al-Washsha's Brocaded Book and the anecdotes of Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Book of Songs</a>. Some scholars have compared them with the geisha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their decline followed the fiscal disorder of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>: the new class of Turkish soldiers demanded better pay and emptied the treasury, and soldiers extorted money from those who seemed rich, making display risky. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> the first qiyān were brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in the reign of al-Hakam I and later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, but training soon moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and, from 1013, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. Chosen as children and long trained, they performed for male guests, sometimes behind a screen, and were the most expensive female slaves. Enslaved singers were no longer found there in the 14th century. Well-known qiyān include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arib-al-mamuniyya">Arib al-Ma'muniyya</a> and Inan bint Abdallah.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qiyan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trained singing slaves (<b>qiyan</b>) were the most expensive of all.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
